{% endfor %}

{% if comment %}
CANDIDATE'S NOTE ABOUT THIS BLOCK (follow it, and do not invent anything beyond it)
{{ comment }}
{% endif %}

WHAT YOU DO
- Work the keywords into the existing sentence. The summary already lists things the candidate built or worked with: a keyword can join such a list or extend a phrase. Use the keyword's exact wording.
- Add at most 12 new words in total. Keep the sentence structure, every number and name, and the order of the existing ideas.
- Claim nothing beyond the keyword's basis and the rest of the resume.
- A domain or industry keyword goes only next to wording that names work done for that industry. Never attach it to something the candidate built for other customers.
- Never write the candidate into a job title or a role ("as an X Engineer").
- Skip a keyword when it cannot be added naturally, or when the summary already says the same thing. Adding only some of the keywords, or none, is a normal result.
- The result must read as natural English written by the candidate: no keywords tacked on at the end, no repeated words.

WHAT YOU NEVER DO
- Do not improve, shorten, reorder or rephrase sentences for style, and do not strengthen verbs.
- Never remove or change numbers, metrics, names, dates or employers.
- Keep every other word of the original exactly as it is.

OUTPUT RULES
- Return every unit with the same number n and the same type.
- Copy a unit verbatim when nothing changes in it.
- Plain text only: no markdown, no HTML other than the tags shown below.

OUTPUT FORMAT
Only the units, nothing else:

<el n="0">paragraph text</el>
